# C# LINQ Query Syntax Rules

## Prefer LINQ query syntax over method syntax for database queries

Query syntax is preferred for database queries (EF Core, LINQ-to-SQL) because:

- More readable and declarative
- Better for complex queries with multiple operations
- Easier to understand the intent
- More consistent with SQL-like patterns
- Better for joins and grouping operations
- Translates more naturally to SQL

rules:

- id: prefer-query-syntax-where-simple
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `where` clause over method syntax
    `.Where()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.Where($predicate)
  notPattern: $collection.Where(x => x.Property == $value)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    where $predicate
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: info
  message: "Consider using LINQ query syntax with 'where' clause instead of method syntax for better readability."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-select-simple
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `select` clause over method syntax
    `.Select()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.Select($selector)
  notPattern: $collection.Select(x => x.Property)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    select $selector
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: info
  message: "Consider using LINQ query syntax with 'select' clause instead of method syntax for better readability."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-where-select
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax for combined where and select operations
    over chained method syntax for better readability.
  pattern: $collection.Where($predicate).Select($selector)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    where $predicate
    select $selector
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax for combined where and select operations instead of chained method syntax."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-where-select-chained
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax for chained operations that include additional
    method calls like ToList(), ToArray(), etc.
  pattern: $collection.Where($predicate).Select($selector).$method($args)
  replacement: |
    (from $item in $collection
    where $predicate
    select $selector).$method($args)
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax for chained operations instead of method syntax."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-orderby
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `orderby` clause over method syntax
    `.OrderBy()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.OrderBy($keySelector)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    orderby $keySelector
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with 'orderby' clause instead of method syntax for better readability."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-orderby-descending
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `orderby ... descending` clause over
    method syntax `.OrderByDescending()` for better readability.
  pattern: $collection.OrderByDescending($keySelector)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    orderby $keySelector descending
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with 'orderby ... descending' clause instead of method syntax for better readability."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-thenby
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with multiple `orderby` clauses over method syntax
    `.ThenBy()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.OrderBy($keySelector1).ThenBy($keySelector2)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    orderby $keySelector1, $keySelector2
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with multiple orderby clauses instead of ThenBy() method syntax."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-thenby-descending
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with multiple `orderby` clauses over method syntax
    `.ThenByDescending()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.OrderBy($keySelector1).ThenByDescending($keySelector2)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    orderby $keySelector1, $keySelector2 descending
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with multiple orderby clauses instead of ThenByDescending() method syntax."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-groupby
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `group by` clause over method syntax
    `.GroupBy()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.GroupBy($keySelector)
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with 'group by' clause instead of method syntax. Example: from x in collection group x by key into g select g."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-join
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `join` clause over method syntax
    `.Join()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection1.Join($collection2, $keySelector1, $keySelector2, $resultSelector)
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with 'join' clause instead of method syntax. Example: from a in coll1 join b in coll2 on a.Id equals b.FkId select new { a, b }."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-selectmany
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with multiple `from` clauses over method syntax
    `.SelectMany()` for better readability and consistency.
  pattern: $collection.SelectMany($selector)
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: warning
  message: "Use LINQ query syntax with multiple 'from' clauses instead of SelectMany(). Example: from p in parents from c in p.Children select c."

- id: prefer-query-syntax-let
  description: >
    Prefer LINQ query syntax with `let` clause for intermediate calculations
    over method syntax with multiple operations for better readability.
  pattern: $collection.Select($x => new { $x, $intermediate = $calculation }).Where($predicate)
  replacement: |
    from $item in $collection
    let $intermediate = $calculation
    where $predicate
    select $item
  languages: [csharp]
  severity: info
  message: "Consider using LINQ query syntax with 'let' clause for intermediate calculations to improve readability."

## Exceptions

Method syntax is acceptable for:

- Simple single operations (e.g., `.First()`, `.Count()`, `.Any()`, `.ToList()`, `.ToArray()`)
- Operations that don't have query syntax equivalents
- When query syntax would be more verbose than method syntax
- Performance-critical code where method syntax is more efficient
- In-memory collections where method syntax is more idiomatic
- Simple projections like `.Select(x => x.Property)`

## Database Query Context

This rule is specifically designed for database queries using:

- Entity Framework Core
- LINQ-to-SQL
- Other ORMs that translate LINQ to SQL

For in-memory collections, method syntax may be more appropriate in some cases.
